course: physics
lesson: 6
title: Friction: the grip that stops and helps
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Friction is the grip between two surfaces that touch: rough surfaces pressed hard together grip a lot, smooth ones hardly at all.
explain: Slide in your socks on a polished floor and you glide; do it in trainers and the rubber grips and stops you at once.
explain: Friction slows things down, but it also lets you walk, stop and hold things, and the bumpy soles of your shoes are made to grip.
explain: Rub your hands together on a cold day and they warm up: friction always turns some movement into heat.
explain: Bike brakes use this too, squeezing rubber pads against the wheel until the speed turns into heat.
fact: Friction is the grip between two touching surfaces; rough surfaces and surfaces pressed hard together grip more.
fact: Ice has a thin slippery layer on top, so skates hardly grip, but scientists still argue about exactly why ice is so slippery.
fact: Shoe soles have bumps that grip the ground and grooves that push away water and mud, and football boots have studs that dig into grass.
fact: Rubbing your hands makes friction, and friction turns movement into heat; a rope rushing through your hands can even burn them.
fact: Bike brakes squeeze rubber pads against the wheel, and the harder you squeeze, the more they grip.
fact: Wet brakes grip less, so you must brake earlier in the rain.
fact: The speed a bike loses when it brakes turns into heat in the pads and rim, which is why they are hot after a long ride downhill.
fact: On long mountain roads car brakes can get so hot that they stop working well, so drivers use low gears.
fact: A layer of water between tyres and road makes a car skid; this is called aquaplaning, and tyre treads push the water away, so worn-smooth "bald" tyres are dangerous in the rain.
fact: Sand and grit on icy paths give shoes and tyres something rough to grip, and salt melts the ice because salty water freezes at a lower temperature.
fact: A slide is faster on a smooth mat or in smooth shorts than in rough jeans, because smooth material rubs less; bare, sweaty legs grip a metal slide, squeak and slow you down.
fact: A match bursts into flame because one quick rub on the rough strip makes enough heat to set off the chemicals on its head.
fact: Heavier things press harder on the floor and so have more friction.
exercise: Why do socks slide on a polished floor but trainers grip? | Rubber grips much more than smooth socks | Socks are heavier than trainers | Polished floors push socks along | Trainers are pulled down by gravity more
exercise: What is friction? | The grip between two touching surfaces | A kind of magnet in the ground | The weight of a moving thing | The wind that slows all moving things down
exercise: Why can you glide far on ice with one push? | Ice is so slippery there is very little friction | Ice pushes skates along by itself | Skates make you much lighter | The cold makes you go faster
exercise: Do scientists fully agree why ice is so slippery? | No, they still argue about the exact reason | Yes, it was settled long ago | Yes, it is only because the skates melt the top | No, because ice is not slippery
exercise: What are the grooves under your shoes for? | Pushing away water and mud so the bumps can grip | Making your shoes lighter to wear | Letting air in to cool your feet | Making a nice pattern in the sand
exercise: Why do football boots have studs? | To dig into the grass for more grip | To make the player stand a little taller | To keep the boots clean and free of mud | To make the ball bounce higher off them
exercise: Where does the warmth come from when you rub your hands? | Friction turns the movement into heat | Your blood comes up to the skin | The air between them gets squashed | Your hands catch heat from the warm room
exercise: How do bike brakes slow the wheel? | Pads rub on the wheel and friction slows it | The pads push the wheel backwards to stop it | Brakes let the air out of the tyre | Brakes make the wheel heavier
exercise: Why do bike brakes work worse in the rain? | Wet pads grip the wheel less | Rain makes the bike heavier | Water fills up the tyres | Rain makes the wheel spin slower
exercise: Where does the bike's speed go when you brake? | Into heat in the pads and rim | Into the air rushing behind the bike | Back up into your legs and feet | Nowhere at all, it just disappears
exercise: Why are bald tyres dangerous in the rain? | They cannot push water away, so the car skids | They are lighter than new tyres | They pop more easily in rain | They make the car go too slowly
exercise: Why do people throw sand on icy paths? | It gives shoes something rough to grip | It makes the ice thicker and safer | It keeps the ice clean | It makes the path warmer and nicer to walk on
exercise: Why does salt help on icy roads? | Salty water freezes at a lower temperature | Salt makes the ice harder | Salt is warm and melts the snow by its heat | Salt is heavy and holds cars down
exercise: Why are slides faster when you sit on a slippery mat? | The smooth mat rubs less on the slide | The mat makes you much heavier | The mat pushes you down the slide | The mat stops the air from slowing you down
exercise: Why must a match be rubbed and not just touched to the box? | The quick rub makes the heat that sets it off | Touching is too gentle to break it | The box only works when it moves | The match needs air to be pushed into it by the rub
exercise: Why does a heavy box rub more on the carpet than a light one? | It presses harder on the floor | It has bigger and sharper corners | Carpets pull harder on heavy things | Light boxes float a little above it
test: whyyy do my hands get warm when i rub em, is it magic | No, friction turns the rubbing into heat | Yes, rubbing makes your hands a bit magnetic | Your hands catch the room's heat | Your blood boils a little
test: In a cartoon a boy brakes his bike hard in the rain and stops at once. What is closer to the truth? | Wet brakes grip less, so he needs longer to stop | Wet brakes grip much better | Rain makes the bike stop by itself | Brakes do not work at all in rain
test: Why do climbers wear gloves when a rope runs through their hands? | A fast rope can burn the skin by friction | Gloves make the rope lighter | Gloves stop the rope getting wet from sweat | Ropes are too cold to touch
test: A heavy box will not slide across the carpet. What trick helps most? | Put it on a rug and drag the rug on a smooth floor | Push it harder from the top | Add more weight on top | Wet the carpet first
test: Why do drivers use low gears on long mountain roads? | So the brakes do not get too hot | So the car goes faster downhill | To save petrol going down | Because high gears break on hills
test: What would happen if you tried to stop quickly on a polished floor in socks? | You would slide on | You would stop at once | You would bounce back | You would stick to the floor
test: What is aquaplaning? | A car sliding on a layer of water under its tyres | A plane landing on the sea | A boat going very fast | A tyre filling up with water
test: Why are matches kept away from children? | One quick rub makes real fire | They are too heavy for children | They taste of chemicals | They break too easily
test: A hot sticky day, bare legs on a metal slide. What happens? | Your skin grips and squeaks and you slow down | You slide twice as fast | The slide gets slippery | Nothing changes on hot days
test: Gran says brakes get hot cos the sun shines on them. right? | No, friction turns the speed into heat | Yes, dark brakes soak up the sunshine all day | Yes, black brakes always get hot | No, brakes get cold when used
test: Which pair has the most friction? | Rubber soles on a rough path | Socks sliding on a polished floor | Skates gliding over smooth ice | A sledge running on fresh snow
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the phenomenon designated friction? | The resistance to sliding between two surfaces in contact | The attraction of bodies towards the centre of the Earth | The tendency of bodies to rise in water | The resistance of the air to all motion
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the brakes of a bicycle are rendered less effective in wet conditions. | Water between pad and rim diminishes their grip | Water increases the mass of the wheel | Water lowers the temperature of the rider's hands | Water causes the tyres to become inflated
exam: It is given that a cyclist descends a long hill with the brakes applied. Ascertain the form into which the energy of motion so lost is principally converted. | Heat in the brake pads and rims | Sound in the air surrounding the bicycle | Light emitted by the pads in braking | Additional mass acquired by the wheels
exam: Determine the reason for which sand is distributed upon icy footpaths. | It furnishes a rough surface affording grip | It increases the thickness of the ice beneath | It raises the temperature of the path | It renders the ice transparent
